displayed as four hours old and read as main having been reset. It had not been;
the reflog and merge-base confirmed the work intact. Third timezone misread today,
same shape each time: a rendered clock trusted as the fact underneath it.

Recorded rather than fixed in the same breath. The defect is in the instrument the
hourly check relies on, so the fix needs a mutation proof that the RENDERED line
carries the zone, not merely that a helper returns one -- the distinction this
session has already got wrong twice.

Second finding: ops/.pretest-poison-counter.json reads {} against a count of 1 at
HEAD, restarting the auto-quarantine countdown for a stalled task's orphaned test.
Who reset it is not established and the entry says so rather than naming a cause.
Not reverted: restoring it by hand writes the conductor's live state from outside.

A pending marker was deleted deliberately. It was armed by this session's own
diagnostic pytest and named ops/.pretest-poison-counter.json as [NOT this run] --
the conductor's in-flight file, not damage from that run. Reverting it is what this
guard has destroyed authored work with three times.

